          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Extreme carries a higher hydrostatic pressure rating — up to 15 psi against the Original's lower figure — and a longer warranty, and it builds slightly thicker per coat. Original is appropriate for walls that are damp and musty with no visible seepage; Extreme is the one to buy if water has actually come through the block in the past.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you apply DRYLOK over painted walls?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, and this is the single biggest cause of failure. DRYLOK works by bonding chemically with open masonry pores, and a film of old paint blocks that reaction entirely. It will look fine for a few months and then lift in sheets. Old paint has to be stripped or ground off before application.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many coats of DRYLOK do you need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Two, always. The published pressure rating and the warranty both assume two full coats. The first should be brushed in hard with a masonry brush to fill the pores, and the second applied 24 hours later in the opposite direction. One coat leaves pinholes and performs as a damp-proofer at best.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does DRYLOK last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "DRYLOK Extreme carries a 15-year warranty on properly prepared masonry and commonly performs well beyond a decade in practice. Its failures are rarely gradual — a correctly applied coating holds for years, while one applied over paint, dust or a damp wall lifts within the first season.",
