    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-it-is" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What DRYLOK Is</a>
          <a href="#does-it-work" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Does It Actually Work?</a>
          <a href="#the-range" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Which Product in the Range</a>
          <a href="#applying" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Applying It Properly</a>
          <a href="#where-it-fails" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Where It Genuinely Fails</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. DRYLOK vs the Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          DRYLOK is a genuinely effective product with an unusually narrow set of conditions, and it is sold in a way that hides how strict those conditions are. Meet them and it works for a decade or more. Miss any one of them and it lifts off the wall in sheets before the next spring.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. What DRYLOK Is
        </h2>

        <p>
          DRYLOK is made by UGL and has been the default interior masonry waterproofer on American hardware store shelves for decades. It is a cementitious latex coating: Portland cement suspended in a latex binder, sold ready-mixed by the gallon and the five-gallon pail.
        </p>

        <p>
          The mechanism is what separates it from masonry paint. Applied to bare, open masonry, the cement fraction reacts with residual moisture and the free lime in the concrete or block, and the coating becomes chemically integrated with the top layer of the wall rather than sitting on it as a film. There is no clean plane between coating and substrate for water pressure to exploit, which is exactly why a published hydrostatic pressure rating is possible at all. The flagship Extreme formula is rated to withstand up to <strong>15 psi</strong> — the pressure water exerts when the soil around a foundation is saturated — and carries a 15-year warranty on properly prepared masonry.
        </p>

        <p>
          Everything that people love and hate about DRYLOK follows from that one design decision. Because the bond is chemical rather than mechanical, it holds far better than any paint under moisture pressure. And because the bond is chemical rather than mechanical, it needs direct contact with bare masonry pores, which makes surface preparation non-negotiable in a way that ordinary coatings are not.
        </p>

        <h2 id="does-it-work" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Does It Actually Work?
        </h2>

        <p>
          For the problem it targets, yes — clearly and repeatably. On a block wall that is damp to the touch, smells musty and blooms white efflorescence each spring, two full coats of properly applied DRYLOK stop the moisture migrating through and keep it stopped for years. We have used it on hollow block and on poured foundation walls, and where the prep was done properly it has held.
        </p>

        <p>
          The complaints are equally real and almost entirely explicable. Search for DRYLOK failures and you find the same photograph over and over: a coating peeling away in large sheets, taking a layer of something with it. That is not a coating that failed under water pressure — that is a coating that never bonded, because it went over old paint, over efflorescence, or over a wall that was still damp when the brush came out.
        </p>

        <p>
          The second category of complaint is more sympathetic: people who applied it correctly and still have water in the basement. In those cases the product did what it could and the diagnosis was wrong. If water arrives after every heavy rain, or appears at the wall-floor joint, or the floor itself is wet, no interior coating solves it, because the water is arriving in volume rather than seeping through pores. That is drainage work, and our <a href="/wet-basement-solutions/">wet basement solutions guide</a> works through the options in order of cost.
        </p>

        <div class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl">
          <h2 id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">What DRYLOK Does — and Doesn't Do</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 text-gray-300">
            <div>
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-400 mb-3">It Does</p>
              <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
                <li>Stop moisture migrating through porous masonry</li>
                <li>Resist real hydrostatic pressure, up to 15 psi</li>
                <li>End the musty smell in a damp basement</li>
                <li>Take a tint, so the wall can be a colour</li>
                <li>Last a decade or more when applied correctly</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF4500] mb-3">It Doesn't</p>
              <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
                <li>Bond over paint, dust or efflorescence</li>
                <li>Bridge or seal a crack</li>
                <li>Fix water arriving from failed drainage</li>
                <li>Work on a wall that is still damp</li>
                <li>Deliver its rating from a single coat</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="the-range" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Which Product in the Range
        </h2>

        <p>
          The shelf holds more DRYLOK products than most people realise, and buying the wrong one is a quieter failure than bad prep.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DRYLOK Extreme</h3>
        <p>
          The flagship, rated to 15 psi with a 15-year warranty. This is the one to buy if water has visibly come through the wall, if the basement has flooded in the past, or if you simply want the best version of the product. It builds slightly thicker per coat than the Original, which also means slightly lower coverage per gallon.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DRYLOK Original (Masonry Waterproofer)</h3>
        <p>
          The long-standing formula, with a lower pressure rating and a shorter warranty at a lower price. Perfectly adequate for a wall that is damp and musty with efflorescence but no active seepage — which describes a large share of older basements. Save the difference and spend it on the preparation tools.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DRYLOK Floor &amp; Wall / Masonry Floor Paint</h3>
        <p>
          A different product for a different surface. Basement floors take abrasion that a wall coating is not formulated for, and using the wall product underfoot leads to wear-through in traffic paths. If the floor is part of the project, buy the floor product — and read our <a href="/basement-floor-sealer/">basement floor sealer guide</a> first, because a slab with moisture coming up through it needs a different approach entirely.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DRYLOK Fast Plug</h3>
        <p>
          Not a coating at all — a rapid-setting hydraulic cement that expands as it cures, used to plug active leaks and fill cracks and holes before the waterproofer goes on. It sets in minutes and will stop water running through a hole while you watch. Anyone using DRYLOK on a wall with any cracks should have a tub of this as well.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">DRYLOK Etch</h3>
        <p>
          A surface preparation product for slick or previously sealed masonry that will not accept the coating. Useful in specific cases, and not a substitute for stripping old paint — etching a painted wall does nothing except make a mess.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Applying It Properly
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the whole review, condensed: preparation is the product. Five steps, and the first three take longer than the painting.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Strip back to bare masonry.</strong> Any existing paint must come off, using a masonry-suitable stripper or a grinder with a dust shroud. Wear proper respiratory protection — silica dust from grinding masonry is genuinely hazardous, not a nuisance.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Remove efflorescence dry.</strong> Wire-brush the white bloom off and vacuum it away. Do not wash it off first: water dissolves the salts and carries them back into the wall, where they recrystallise later and can push your new coating off from behind.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Fill cracks and holes.</strong> Hydraulic cement, chased into a V-groove so the repair has something to grip, or a polyurethane injection kit for an actively leaking crack in poured concrete. Let repairs cure fully. Our <a href="/basement-crack-repair/">basement crack repair guide</a> covers which crack needs which treatment.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Brush the first coat, do not roll it.</strong> The first coat's job is to fill the pores, and a roller bridges over them. Use a stiff masonry brush and work the material in with pressure, in overlapping horizontal strokes. It is slow and physical and it is the step that makes the coating perform.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Second coat after 24 hours.</strong> Apply in vertical strokes, crossing the first, which makes any misses obvious. This coat can be rolled. Skipping it means you have applied a damp-proofer, not a waterproofer, and voided the warranty in the process.
        </p>

        <p>
          Budget realistically for coverage while you are planning. On block, expect roughly 75 to 100 square feet per gallon per coat, which is far less than a normal paint and catches people out on the second trip to the store. A basement perimeter of any size runs to sixteen or twenty gallons across the two coats, and buying it all at once avoids a colour or batch mismatch halfway along a wall.
        </p>

        <h2 id="where-it-fails" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Where It Genuinely Fails
        </h2>

        <p>
          Setting aside preparation, three situations are outside what any version of this product can do.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Walls that move.</strong> DRYLOK cures rigid. A wall that opens hairline cracks seasonally will crack the coating along the same line, and no amount of extra coats changes that. That is elastomeric membrane territory, and the trade-offs are covered in our <a href="/liquid-rubber-vs-drylok/">liquid rubber vs DRYLOK comparison</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Water under real volume.</strong> Fifteen psi is a meaningful rating and it is still a coating on the dry side of a wall. Against a failed perimeter drain or a water table that rises above the slab, water finds the weakest point, and an interior coating is the last thing in its path.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Walls that never dry.</strong> Application requires a dry substrate, and some basements genuinely never provide one. Where a wall stays damp regardless of season, a product rated for damp application is a better bet than fighting the condition.
        </p>

        <h2 id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. DRYLOK vs the Alternatives
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Versus Flex Seal.</strong> Not a real comparison, despite how often the two come up together. Flex Seal patches defined leaks on almost any material; DRYLOK coats whole masonry walls. Using either for the other's job wastes money, and our <a href="/drylok-vs-flex-seal/">DRYLOK vs Flex Seal breakdown</a> explains why.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Versus liquid rubber.</strong> A genuine head-to-head. The membrane wins on any wall that moves and on the exterior face of a foundation; DRYLOK wins on cost per square foot, on being paintable afterwards, and on carrying a published pressure rating.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Versus acrylic masonry waterproofers.</strong> KILZ and Behr's basement products apply more easily and leave a smoother, more finished-looking wall, at the cost of pressure resistance. For a dry-to-damp wall in a room being finished, they are a reasonable choice; for a wall that has actually been wet, they are not.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Versus penetrating reactive sealers.</strong> Products like RadonSeal soak into dense poured concrete and seal it from within with nothing on the surface. On dense poured walls they are excellent and permanent; on porous block they have too little to react with. Our <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-paint/">basement waterproofing paint guide</a> puts all four categories side by side.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes With DRYLOK</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying It Over Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The defining failure. The chemical bond needs bare masonry, and any paint film prevents it. The coating looks perfect for a few months and then peels in sheets — and the product takes the blame for a skipped step.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling the First Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A roller bridges pores instead of filling them, leaving pinholes for water to find. The first coat has to be worked in with a stiff masonry brush; the second can be rolled once the pores are already filled.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping at One Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 15 psi rating and the warranty both assume two full coats. One coat looks finished and performs like a damp-proofer, which is exactly the gap people notice the following spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over an Unfilled Crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                DRYLOK seals pores, not gaps. Paint spanning a crack cracks with it. Fill with hydraulic cement — Fast Plug is made for this — or inject an active leak, and let the repair cure first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using It to Avoid Fixing the Gutters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the basement is wet after every storm, the cheapest fix is outside: clean gutters, downspout extensions and soil graded away from the foundation. A coating applied before those is money spent on a symptom.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Verdict -->
        <div id="verdict" class="my-16 bg-gradient-to-br from-gray-950 to-gray-900 rounded-3xl p-8 lg:p-12 text-white shadow-2xl relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-[#FF4500] text-white font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest px-4 py-2 rounded-bl-xl">
            ★ Our Verdict
          </div>
          <h2 id="our-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-white mt-0 mb-6 border-none">DRYLOK: Our Verdict</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 text-gray-300">
            <p>
              <strong class="text-green-400">Buy it if:</strong> your basement walls are damp, musty and blooming efflorescence, the masonry is bare or you are willing to strip it, and there is no standing water. In that situation it is the best-value meaningful improvement available for a basement, and the Extreme formula's rating and warranty are real.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-[#FF4500]">Skip it if:</strong> the wall cracks seasonally, the masonry never dries out, the wall is painted and you are not prepared to strip it, or water arrives in volume after rain. Each of those points at a different product or a different job entirely.
            </p>
            <p class="text-lg border-t border-gray-700 pt-6 mt-6">
              <strong class="text-white">The Honest Bottom Line:</strong> DRYLOK deserves both its reputation and its complaints. It is a genuinely capable waterproofer sold to a mass audience with a preparation requirement most of that audience does not read — and the gap between the two is where every peeling photograph comes from. Do the stripping, brush the first coat in hard, apply the second, and it will hold for a decade. Skip any of that and the tin was never the problem.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does DRYLOK actually work?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For moisture migrating through porous masonry — damp walls, musty smell, efflorescence — yes, reliably, when applied in two coats to bare prepared masonry. It does not solve water arriving under pressure from failed drainage, and most complaints about it trace back to preparation rather than the coating.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Original or Extreme?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Extreme has the higher pressure rating (15 psi), the longer warranty and a slightly thicker build. Original suits walls that are damp and musty with no visible seepage. If water has actually come through the block before, buy Extreme.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply it over painted walls?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No — this is the single biggest cause of failure. The coating bonds chemically with open masonry pores and paint blocks that entirely. It will look fine briefly and then peel in sheets. Strip or grind the paint off first.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Two, always. Both the pressure rating and the warranty assume it. Brush the first in hard with a masonry brush to fill the pores, wait 24 hours, then apply the second in the opposite direction so misses show up.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it last?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Extreme carries a 15-year warranty on properly prepared masonry and commonly performs beyond a decade. Failures are rarely gradual — a correct application holds for years, while one over paint, dust or damp lifts within the first season.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over DRYLOK?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, once fully cured, using a masonry-suitable topcoat — though it is tintable at the store, so painting over it is often unnecessary. Allow the full cure time on the label before topcoating.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will it stop a leaking crack?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. It seals pores, not gaps, so over an unfilled crack it simply cracks too. Fill with hydraulic cement first — DRYLOK's own Fast Plug is made for this — or inject an actively leaking crack in poured concrete, and let the repair cure before coating.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth it over cheap masonry paint?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For any wall with a moisture problem, yes — ordinary masonry paint carries no pressure rating and is decorative rather than protective. For a dry wall you simply want to look better, cheaper masonry paint does that and the premium buys nothing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

